3.7.m:
Having cupped the hands together, one should direct them to the left nostril;
curling the middle finger on the right, one should likewise bend both thumbs.
Then, having formed the shape of a trident, one should turn it towards the heart;
this is remembered as the mudrā of Aghora, destroying all obstacles.

and one hundred is stated for the Rucaka, and ten less [than a hundred] for the Śaśaka.
A width of twelve aṅgulas is called a tāla.
The height of the foot from the ankle is proclaimed to be four aṅgulas;
and the two shanks are two tālas, and the two knees are equal to the foot.
And the two thighs are equal to the shanks; the navel from the penis is one tāla;
and from the navel to the heart is the same, and from the heart to the throat;
the neck should be a third of a tāla, and the face should be a tāla;
and the crown above the forehead is stated to be a sixth of a tāla.
The penis should be known to be in the middle: thus is length proclaimed.
The hand is declared to be a tāla, O king; the upper arms are seventeen aṅgulas;
the forearms are declared to be just as much; half of the chest is eight [aṅgulas].
This is declared as the measure in length of the Haṃsa, O king.
According to this same method one should calculate for the others as well;
in length and circumference they are all symmetrical, O ruler of men.

3.8.m:
An excessive one produces sorrow, and a wretched one [does the same], O best of kings; / a dry one leads to loss of wealth, and an angry one magnifies fear.
An image with an emaciated belly should not be made, nor one with an overly protruding belly; / nor should one with a wound be fashioned, O descendant of Yadu;
nor one deficient or excessive in proportions, nor of a dry complexion, / nor with an open mouth, nor bent, O delighter of the Yadus.

A person lacking proper proportion might suffer on earth by the force of time and emotion; / reflecting thus with his own intellect, the wise man should determine proportion in the application of diminution and increase.
Mārkaṇḍeya said: / Having mixed brick powder of three kinds with a third part of clay, / [one should add] bdellium resin, along with beeswax, licorice, muruka, and molasses;
having combined safflower with oil, one should add an equal part [of it]; / one should pulverise there a third part of lime burnt by fire;
having mixed that with two parts of bilva fruit, and adding an ink-like astringent, / the intelligent man should then add a portion of sand according to requirement;
then he should steep it with slimy bark-decoction water, / and leave that entirely moistened [mixture] to stand for just a month.
Having carefully taken it out when it has attained softness after a month, / the skilled man should apply the plaster on the wall, inspecting it once dry,
smooth, even, well-adherent, free from depressions and elevations, / neither too thick nor too thin.

having drawn [the outline], the wise [painter] should establish [it] in proportion and in posture;
then he should colour it with pigments according to the requirements of the place.
Whether his complexion is dark or fair, he should display that [complexion];
and its characteristic has been stated at length, O king, when it arises.
Five primary colours are recorded: white, yellow, in reverse order,
black, and blue, O best of kings; hundreds are known through mixtures.
By the division of the primary colours and through the conception of the mood,
he should produce colours by the hundreds and thousands through his own intellect.
A prominent mixture among blues is praised as 'pālāśa' [leaf-green];
it is pure, mixed with white, and also [one] where blue predominates.
And one [colour] exceeds [another] in accordance with the complexions;
whether white predominates, is lesser, or [they are] in equal parts, it is thus threefold.
Combined with a single binding medium, it is thus differentiated in manifold ways;
